## The concept

```js
function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;                    // scope ends when makeCounter returns...
  return () => ++count;             // ...but this function keeps it alive
}

const next = makeCounter();
next();  // 1
next();  // 2
```

The inner function holds a live reference to `count`, not a copy of its value.

---

## Why React makes this unavoidable

Every render is a fresh function call with fresh `const` bindings:

```jsx
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // A NEW function, closing over THIS render's `count`
  function handleClick() {
    setTimeout(() => alert(count), 3000);
  }

  return <button onClick={handleClick}>{count}</button>;
}
```

Click when count is 0, then click three more times before the alert fires. It alerts **0**, not 3 — the closure captured render 0's `count`.

This is not a bug. It is what makes React predictable: each render is a consistent snapshot where nothing changes underneath you mid-execution.

---

## Where it becomes a real problem

```jsx
// ❌ The interval callback is created once, closing over count = 0 forever
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => setCount(count + 1), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);

// ✅ The updater form does not read count at all
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => setCount((c) => c + 1), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);
```

---

## The three fixes

| Fix | When |
|-----|------|
| Functional updater `setX(prev => …)` | The new value derives from the old — best option |
| Add the value to the dependency array | The effect genuinely should restart |
| A ref holding the latest value | The callback must be stable but read fresh values |

---

## Where closures also show up

**`var` in a loop** — the classic pre-`let` interview question:

```js
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) setTimeout(() => console.log(i));  // 3, 3, 3
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) setTimeout(() => console.log(i));  // 0, 1, 2
```

`var` is function-scoped, so all three callbacks share one binding. `let` is block-scoped and creates a fresh binding per iteration — exactly the mechanism React relies on for per-render state.

> Understanding closures is not optional React knowledge. Almost every confusing hook bug reduces to "which render's variable is this function looking at?"

## useState

```tsx
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);          // inferred: number
const [name, setName] = useState('');            // inferred: string

// ❌ Inferred as null — you can never set a user
const [user, setUser] = useState(null);

// ✅ Tell it the full type
const [user, setUser] = useState<User | null>(null);
const [items, setItems] = useState<Item[]>([]);   // otherwise never[]
```

The rule: **add a generic whenever the initial value does not represent every value the state can hold.**

---

## useRef — two distinct forms

```tsx
// DOM ref — React writes to it, so `current` is read-only to you
const inputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);
inputRef.current?.focus();

// Mutable value — you write to it, so `current` is writable
const timerRef = useRef<number | undefined>(undefined);
timerRef.current = window.setInterval(tick, 1000);
```

Passing `null` as the initial value selects the read-only overload — a deliberate design so you cannot accidentally reassign a DOM ref React owns.

---

## Events

```tsx
function Form() {
  const onChange = (e: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => {
    setValue(e.target.value);       // typed as string
  };

  const onSubmit = (e: React.FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>) => {
    e.preventDefault();
  };

  const onClick = (e: React.MouseEvent<HTMLButtonElement>) => { };
  const onKeyDown = (e: React.KeyboardEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => {
    if (e.key === 'Enter') submit();
  };
}
```

Inline handlers are inferred, so annotation is only needed for handlers defined separately:

```tsx
// No annotation required — TypeScript infers it from the JSX attribute
<input onChange={(e) => setValue(e.target.value)} />
```

---

## useReducer

```tsx
type State = { count: number };
type Action = { type: 'inc' } | { type: 'set'; value: number };

function reducer(state: State, action: Action): State {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'inc': return { count: state.count + 1 };
    case 'set': return { count: action.value };   // `value` narrowed here
  }
}

const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, { count: 0 });
```

The discriminated union is what gives you exhaustiveness checking and narrowing inside each case.

---

## Context

```tsx
const AuthContext = createContext<AuthValue | null>(null);

export function useAuth(): AuthValue {
  const ctx = useContext(AuthContext);
  if (!ctx) throw new Error('useAuth must be used within AuthProvider');
  return ctx;   // narrowed to non-null after the guard
}
```

The throw does double duty: a useful runtime error, and it narrows the type so callers never deal with `null`.

## The three

```js
const nums = [1, 2, 3, 4];

nums.map((n) => n * 2);                    // [2, 4, 6, 8]      same length
nums.filter((n) => n % 2 === 0);           // [2, 4]            subset
nums.reduce((sum, n) => sum + n, 0);       // 10                one value
```

None of them mutate the original. `push`, `splice` and `sort` do — which is why the first three appear constantly in React and the others need care.

---

## In React

```jsx
// map — render a list
{users.map((user) => <Row key={user.id} user={user} />)}

// filter — remove an item immutably
setTodos(todos.filter((t) => t.id !== id));

// map — update one item immutably
setTodos(todos.map((t) => (t.id === id ? { ...t, done: true } : t)));

// reduce — derive a total
const total = items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0);
```

That third pattern — `map` with a conditional spread — is the canonical immutable single-item update. It is worth being able to write from memory.

---

## reduce for grouping

```js
const byCategory = products.reduce((acc, p) => {
  (acc[p.category] ??= []).push(p);
  return acc;
}, {});
// { books: [...], toys: [...] }
```

Modern JavaScript also has `Object.groupBy(products, p => p.category)` for exactly this.

---

## Common mistakes

```jsx
// ❌ forEach returns undefined
{users.forEach((u) => <Row user={u} />)}

// ❌ A block body with no return
{users.map((u) => { <Row user={u} />; })}

// ✅ Implicit return
{users.map((u) => <Row key={u.id} user={u} />)}

// ✅ Explicit return when you need statements
{users.map((u) => {
  const label = format(u);
  return <Row key={u.id} label={label} />;
})}
```

---

## The chaining trap

```jsx
// Each call walks the array again — fine for 100 items, wasteful for 100,000
items.filter(isActive).map(toRow).sort(byName)
```

Readability usually wins at typical UI sizes. For genuinely large arrays, a single `reduce` or a plain loop does one pass.

---

## The immutable additions worth knowing

`toSorted()`, `toReversed()`, `toSpliced()` and `with()` are non-mutating counterparts to `sort`, `reverse`, `splice` and index assignment — now widely supported:

```js
setItems(items.toSorted(byName));         // instead of [...items].sort(byName)
setItems(items.with(2, updatedItem));     // instead of a map with an index check
```

## The effect mistake

```jsx
// ❌ An async function returns a promise. React expects a cleanup function.
useEffect(async () => {
  const data = await fetchData();
  setData(data);
}, []);

// ✅ Declare it inside and call it
useEffect(() => {
  let cancelled = false;

  (async () => {
    const data = await fetchData();
    if (!cancelled) setData(data);
  })();

  return () => { cancelled = true; };
}, []);
```

The `cancelled` flag stops a late response from setting state after the effect has been torn down.

---

## Sequential when it should be parallel

```js
// ❌ 900ms — each await blocks the next
const user = await fetchUser();       // 300ms
const posts = await fetchPosts();     // 300ms
const tags = await fetchTags();       // 300ms

// ✅ 300ms — all three start immediately
const [user, posts, tags] = await Promise.all([
  fetchUser(), fetchPosts(), fetchTags(),
]);
```

Only sequence them when a later request genuinely needs an earlier result. This waterfall is one of the most common real performance problems in React apps.

---

## The Promise combinators

| Method | Behaviour |
|--------|-----------|
| `Promise.all` | All succeed, or reject on the first failure |
| `Promise.allSettled` | Waits for all; returns status for each |
| `Promise.race` | First to settle, success or failure |
| `Promise.any` | First to **succeed** |

`allSettled` is the right choice when partial failure is acceptable — three dashboard widgets where one failing should not blank the page.

---

## Unhandled rejections

```jsx
// ❌ A rejection here is silent — no error boundary catches it
useEffect(() => { fetchData().then(setData); }, []);

// ✅
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData().then(setData).catch(setError);
}, []);
```

Error boundaries do not catch async errors, because the throw happens outside the render stack.

---

## fetch does not reject on HTTP errors

```js
// ❌ A 500 response resolves normally — `data` will be an error page
const data = await fetch(url).then((r) => r.json());

// ✅ Check ok explicitly
const res = await fetch(url);
if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
const data = await res.json();
```

`fetch` only rejects on network failure. This surprises people coming from axios, which throws on non-2xx by default.

---

## await in a loop

```js
// ❌ Sequential — n × latency
for (const id of ids) results.push(await fetchItem(id));

// ✅ Parallel
const results = await Promise.all(ids.map(fetchItem));
```

The sequential version is occasionally what you want — rate limiting, or ordered writes — but it should be a deliberate choice.
